  "result": "No, that's not right. A language model doesn't remember anything between conversations. Each request starts blank, and the model only sees the text your app sends it.\n\nThis is easy to get wrong because chatbots feel like they remember. What's actually happening is that the app re-sends the earlier messages with every new request. Anything not in that text doesn't exist for the model.\n\nFor your assistant, last week's training status will only be known if your code does two things:\n1. Saves it somewhere, such as a database or a JSON file keyed by student.\n2. Loads it and puts it into the prompt at the start of the next session.\n\nSo \"memory\" is something you build around the model. Deciding what to save and what to load back is the memory design in your mission.\n\nIf your assistant ran a month of sessions and you never saved anything, what would it know about a returning student?",
